+Someone drops a cup and says \mention{Oops!}; someone catches a finger
+in a drawer and says \mention{Ouch!}. Either reaction can also accompany
+a clause. The following constructed pairs illustrate the two environments.
+
+\ea\label{ex:core-distribution}
+  \ea \exmention{Oops!}\qquad\exmention{Oops, I've dropped it!}
+  \ex \exmention{Ouch!}\qquad\exmention{Ouch, that hurt!}
+  \z
+\z
+
+In the independent utterances, the lexical item supplies the expression
+without an unspoken clause. In the longer examples, it functions as
+a supplement to the clause. \mention{I've dropped it} and \mention{that
+hurt} are already complete: the reaction fills no argument or integrated
+modifier position within them. These are two characteristic environments
+for the grammatical category \term{interjection}.
+
+I take \mention{ah}, \mention{hey}, \mention{oh}, \mention{oops},
+\mention{ouch}, \mention{sh}, \mention{ugh}, and \mention{wow}, in
+their conventional interjectional uses, as a working core. \textit{CGEL}
+identifies these as central members and attributes both independent
+and supplementary use to them \citep[1361]{HuddlestonPullum2002}.
+Its additional example \mention{alas} illustrates how a dated form
+can have a secure grammatical assignment. Here, \term{core} concerns
+the support for those assignments, rather than frequency or typicality.
+
+The shared profile combines these positive possibilities with restricted
+integration. The selected uses are characteristically invariant and don't
+participate in the ordinary argument, predicative, and modifier patterns
+of nouns, verbs, and adjectives. \textcite[105--106]{ameka1992} describes
+primary interjections through their independent utterance capacity, limited
+combination, and lack of productive inflection. \textcite[124--125]{wilkins1992}
+likewise identifies a formal profile of conventional lexical utterances,
+keeping their semantic and pragmatic properties available for separate
+investigation.
